        "char_start": 781,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 436,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 437,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: Analyze the phase shift.\nThe phase shift is determined by the horizontal shift of the sine wave. The standard sine function $y = \\sin(x)$ starts at $(0,0)$. In our function, the sine wave starts at a point where it crosses the midline (which is $y = 1$) and moves downward. This suggests a phase shift. The general form of the sine function with a phase shift is $y = a \\sin(bx + c) + d$. The phase shift is given by $-\\frac{c}{b}$. Since the sine wave starts at a point where it crosses the midline and moves downward, this indicates a phase shift of $\\pi$ units to the left. Thus, we have:\n\\[\n-\\frac{c}{3} = \\pi \\implies c = -3\\pi\n\\]\nSince $c$ must be a positive constant, we take the absolute value:\n\\[\nc = 3\\pi\n\\]"
      },
